From: DashBerry <zero/README.md>
Subject: [PATCH] uvc: report where the start-up gap goes

Three traces now show the daemon silent for 10-15 s between

    DEVICE_V4L2: STREAM ON success
    DEVICE_UVC:  STREAM ON success

with nothing logged in between. A 23 s capture that only begins 15 s in
produces no usable frames, which is exactly what the host reports:

    h264parse0: No valid frames found before end of stream

On the V4L2 path, UVC streaming starts at the FIRST frame handed over from the
sensor, so the gap looked like the camera being slow to deliver. It is not:
capturing straight off /dev/video0 with

    v4l2-ctl --set-fmt-video=width=1280,height=720,pixelformat=H264 \
             --stream-mmap=N --stream-count=30 --stream-to=/dev/null

starts immediately at both N=2 and N=8. So the sensor is fast, the buffer count
is not the variable, and where those seconds go is genuinely unknown.

Rather than guess a further time, measure. Two lines, printed once per stream:

  - large first number  -> the wait is BEFORE the dequeue: select(), or the
                           sensor as THIS process configures it, which differs
                           from the v4l2-ctl case in at least sizeimage
  - small first, large second -> the wait is on the UVC side

The timestamps in the journal already bound the total; this says which half
owns it, which is the one thing three traces have not been able to say.
--- a/uvc-gadget.c
+++ b/uvc-gadget.c
@@ -741,6 +741,32 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
+/* DashBerry: where the start-up gap goes.
+ *
+ * Between "DEVICE_V4L2: STREAM ON success" and "DEVICE_UVC: STREAM ON success"
+ * the daemon has been silent for 10-15 s on three separate traces, and a 23 s
+ * capture that only begins 15 s in produces no usable frames at all.
+ *
+ * On the V4L2 path UVC streaming starts at the FIRST frame handed over, so the
+ * gap looked like the camera being slow — but capturing straight off
+ * /dev/video0 with v4l2-ctl, at both 2 and 8 buffers, starts immediately. So
+ * the sensor is not slow, and where those seconds actually go is unknown.
+ *
+ * These two lines split it. Both large means the wait is BEFORE the dequeue,
+ * i.e. in select() or the sensor as this process has configured it; a small
+ * first and a large second means the wait is on the UVC side. One number
+ * settles which half to look at, and it prints once per stream rather than
+ * per frame.
+ */
+static double dashberry_now_ms(void)
+{
+    struct timeval tv;
+    gettimeofday(&tv, NULL);
+    return (tv.tv_sec + tv.tv_usec * 1e-6) * 1000.0;
+}
+
+double dashberry_v4l2_on_ms = 0;
+
 static void v4l2_uvc_video_process()
 {
     struct v4l2_buffer vbuf;
@@ -763,6 +789,11 @@
 
     v4l2_dev.dqbuf_count++;
 
+    if (v4l2_dev.dqbuf_count == 1 && dashberry_v4l2_on_ms > 0) {
+        printf("TIMING: first sensor frame dequeued %.0f ms after V4L2 STREAM ON\n",
+               dashberry_now_ms() - dashberry_v4l2_on_ms);
+    }
+
     /* Queue video buffer to UVC domain. */
     CLEAR(ubuf);
     ubuf.type      = uvc_dev.buffer_type;
@@ -783,6 +814,11 @@
 
     uvc_dev.qbuf_count++;
 
+    if (uvc_dev.qbuf_count == 1 && dashberry_v4l2_on_ms > 0) {
+        printf("TIMING: first frame queued to UVC %.0f ms after V4L2 STREAM ON\n",
+               dashberry_now_ms() - dashberry_v4l2_on_ms);
+    }
+
     if (!uvc_dev.is_streaming) {
         uvc_video_stream(STREAM_ON);
         settings.blink_on_startup = 0;
@@ -1269,6 +1305,7 @@
 
         /* Start V4L2 capturing now. */
         v4l2_video_stream(STREAM_ON);
+        dashberry_v4l2_on_ms = dashberry_now_ms();
     }
 
     if (uvc_request_bufs(uvc_dev.nbufs) < 0) {
